{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgHxdp1YSbdtiEbNmqQ7p","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, the README and the comment in `sync.sh` say it rewrites `tracker.json` every hour. But the script in this copy does not do that. Its whole body is `echo \"sync: no tracker configured in this copy\" >&2`, so it only prints a message and leaves the file alone. The real export must run from a different copy, or the cron entry is not installed here. I haven't checked the crontab on this machine.\n\nIf the real sync does rewrite the file, the test should still pass tomorrow, because it recounts the open issues from the same file that `weekly_report()` reads. Three things can still break it:\n\n- **Race with the writer.** If the test reads while `sync.sh` is writing, it can see a partial file and fail on `json.load`. The test would then fail intermittently at the hour mark. Writing to a temp file and renaming it into place would prevent this. I can't control that from the test.\n- **Export format change.** If the export renames `state` or changes `\"open\"` to something else, both the report and the test change together. The test still passes, so it won't flag the change. Only a check on the real data's shape would catch it.\n- **Same logic on both sides.** The test repeats the counting rule instead of checking a fixed value. My mutation check showed it catches a broken `open_count`. It would not catch a wrong rule that both sides share.\n\nIf you want the test to catch real-data problems, I can make it check that `tracker.json` has the expected fields and values before comparing the counts. I can also check the crontab here and confirm which sync script actually runs."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":527,"cache_read_input_tokens":41934,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_t